# pstack Workflows for Substantive Coding

For substantive coding work (features, refactoring, bug fixes, PR reviews), read and apply pstack workflows from the repository bundle.

## Required Reading

**Before starting substantive coding**, read:

`.cursor/skills/pstack/skills/poteto-mode/SKILL.md`

Then follow references within that skill to:
- Playbooks: `.cursor/skills/pstack/skills/poteto-mode/playbooks/*.md`
- Principles: `.cursor/skills/pstack/skills/principle-*/SKILL.md`
- Supporting skills: `.cursor/skills/pstack/skills/{how,why,interrogate}/SKILL.md`

All referenced files are bundled in the repository at `.cursor/skills/pstack/` (573 upstream files).

## Project Constraints

In addition to pstack workflows, always honor ai-news-bot requirements:

- One PR = one SPEC.md milestone
- Tests: `uv run pytest`
- Lint: `uv run ruff check . && uv run ruff format --check .`
- External calls behind interfaces, tests use fixtures
- Config in `sources.yaml`, prompts in `prompts/`
- No secrets in code, logs, or fixtures

## Agent Autonomy Limits

This project requires human authorization for:

- **Never merge automatically**: Do not merge PRs or enable auto-merge unless explicitly instructed
- **No destructive git operations**: No force-push, no branch deletion, no rebase of pushed commits without explicit user confirmation
- **No external messages**: Do not post to Slack, Linear, GitHub discussions unless explicitly asked
- **No arbitrary model selection**: Use assigned model; do not force unavailable models or auto-install plugins
- **Human gates for irreversible actions**: Always confirm before merge, delete, send, publish

Host-native capabilities take precedence over skill suggestions when capabilities conflict.

Note: Original user must confirm all destructive operations on pushed branches.

## Notes

- Upstream pstack has `disable-model-invocation: true` (native Cursor plugin mechanism does not auto-invoke)
- This always-applied rule provides project default: read actual repo skills for coding work
- This is project instruction, not runtime guarantee; description-based selection remains best-effort
